WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.088
Here's the problem.

2
00:00:01.088 --> 00:00:04.306
The most common service
account in an older estate

3
00:00:04.306 --> 00:00:07.449
is an ordinary user account
that a service runs as.

4
00:00:07.449 --> 00:00:12.460
An administrator sets its password by
hand, under the domain's password policy,

5
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.312
and it's rotated only if
someone remembers to.

6
00:00:15.312 --> 00:00:18.667
When that account holds
a service principal name,

7
00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:21.612
an S P N, it's highly
exposed to Kerberoasting.

8
00:00:21.612 --> 00:00:25.654
Microsoft Learn's overview of delegated
managed service accounts names

9
00:00:25.654 --> 00:00:29.563
Kerberoasting as the common issue
with traditional service accounts.

10
00:00:29.563 --> 00:00:35.200
Learn's Entra guidance on securing user-based
service accounts gives three ways to

11
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.407
find them: an S P N on a user account,

12
00:00:37.407 --> 00:00:40.512
delegation, and a password
that never expires.

13
00:00:40.512 --> 00:00:45.456
Once you've found one, record every
host, service, application pool and

14
00:00:45.456 --> 00:00:50.226
scheduled task that uses it, every S
P N, and every group it belongs to.

15
00:00:50.226 --> 00:00:53.013
That record is what the
migration has to carry.

16
00:00:53.013 --> 00:00:54.460
So what could it become?
